PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7.4/10 (bar 8), 0 level(s) below 6/10: A well-paced 120-level set with clean one-idea-per-chapter introductions and a very good first chapter. Weaknesses: colour order (L56 and L59 use 3-4 colours before L61 teaches them), repeated "spent count" and "bound the ?" tricks in the tally, crack and colour-tally chapters, flat late difficulty with finales no harder than neighbours, and a few outlier difficulties (L87, L23, L78, L81). No level is unfair or has a trap; all are unique by line logic with trap rate 0. Curve: Strong skeleton: 10 chapters of 12, each adding one idea (tally, colour, void, stamp, colour-cross, smudge, crack, colour-tally, then a mixed finale). The opening is excellent. L1-L3 go full line, exact fit, then overlap. All three clear 80% casual. Each chapter then runs teach, practise, boss, breather, twist, finale. L8's wide-gap twist is a real aha. L12 is a fair first masterwork.

Problems:
1. Colour order is broken. L56 (4 colours) and L59 (3 colours) arrive before Conservatory L61 teaches 3-colour colour-cross, so the colour-cross reasoning is used before it is taught.
2. Chapters repeat one trick. The Inkwell tally levels (L15-17, 20-22) are all "count met, rest is paper". The Kiln levels (L85-88, 91-92) are all "bound the ? between walls". Observatory colour-tally (L97-100, 103) repeats the same spent-count idea. Fewer, sharper levels would feel fresher.
3. The late curve flat-lines. From about L81 casual wins sit at 52-60% almost everywhere. Averaged over L81-120 the last third is about 63%, so it is on target. But finales (L96 56%, L108 54%, L120 54%) are no harder than ordinary levels, so there are no real peaks. Bosses and finales should sit 8-12 points below their chapter's average.
4. Isolated easy outliers in the hard zone: L87 (96% casual, 100% skilled), L91, L103 and L115. L87 should not be a breather. It also breaks the skilled 50-95% band, as do L91, L103 and L115 (96-97%).
5. Mid-chapter dips look like spikes against neighbours: L23 (78%), L78 (62%), L81 (61%) and L83 (59%).
6. Mid-game finales (L36 75%, L48 67%) are brittle: L36 is hard but L37 resets to 93%. That works as a chapter reset. It does mean hard levels in the middle (L36 75%, L42 70%) are tougher than some of the late 'twist' levels.
7. Breather cadence is fine. Early sections have no more than 2 easy levels in a row, which passes.
8. Chapter introductions are mostly clean. Void (L37), stamp (L49), smudge (L73) and crack (L85) each arrive alone. The Grand Exhibit then opens straight into combines at L109 with no refresher.

Overall: polished and calm, and the teaching voice is good. It needs more variety late and a higher peak at finales.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L56 (6/10): It jumps to 4 colours at L56 before 3 colours are taught at L61. The single full-width 'd' row at row 11 is clutter that adds nothing, and the 13x13 board is large for a mid-chapter practise. → Drop to 2-3 colours (merge coral and the tip into the beak colour). Remove the 'd' ground line or turn it into a real branch. Move the 4-colour version to the late game.
L59 (6/10): It is the first 3-colour level, at L59, two chapters before colour-cross is taught. The whole bottom row is a single flat 'c' strip that is plain filler. → Re-colour to 2 colours (merge beak and water). Or swap with a Conservatory level and put a 3-colour version after L61.
L87 (6/10): 96% casual and 100% skilled at L87 is an outlier in the last third and breaks the skilled 50-95% band. The vase also repeats 'bound the ? by the short columns'. → Make the crack unbounded by rows alone: remove one wall column's clue help so the player must use both row 8-9 fits and the column heights. Aim for about 70% casual.
L11 (7/10): The idea is a chain of overlaps that follows L9 and L10 closely. The aha is modest, and the castle half is a plain solid block. → Open the castle with a crenellated skyline so row 1-2 edges need one more deduction. Or swap this for a second twist-style gap level.
L16 (7/10): It is a second 'tally met' level right after L15. The two-directions idea is slight, because the quill row is a simple count of a repeating pattern. → Make the second row's gap non-obvious, e.g. break the quill spacing so one quill is off-rhythm. Or cut a quill row to reduce busywork.
L19 (7/10): The picture is small and the deductions are only exact-fit and overlap again, so it feels like filler. No tally appears in a tally chapter. → Add a ringed tally on the tail row so the breather still revisits the chapter idea, or enlarge the swallow to 11x8 with a clearer silhouette.
L23 (7/10): At 78% casual it is a spike against L22 (85%) and it comes straight before the finale. The off-centre frond is mostly thin diagonal lines with few anchors. → Add one more sure overlap row, e.g. widen a leaflet so rows 7-8 give more starting ink. That lifts casual toward 85%.
L27 (7/10): The colour-change insight is repeated from L25-L26, and the radish is a mirror-symmetric leaf-and-root shape with a lot of empty edge columns. → Offset the leaf fan to one side and make the root longer on one side so the spare cell matters in a second column too.
L31 (7/10): The balloon is symmetric and reads as busywork. It is 92% casual but has 22 lines open, which is a lot of reading for a breather. → Shrink to about 8x10 or add an asymmetric flag or rope detail. That gives fewer lines and one small aha.
L39 (7/10): Void is barely used. The deduction is a plain tally-style row-4 insight, and the intent leans on the void only for the silhouette. → Make a void cell sit inside the eye rows so the void shortens row 4's span and part of the solve depends on it.
L40 (7/10): The single interior void at the volute eye is a good idea, but it is a one-cell moment inside an otherwise ordinary level. → Add a second interior void on the other volute, offset, so both sides require different void reads.
L43 (7/10): At 91% casual it is the fourth Sculpture Hall level with the same 'fill a framed row' opening. The pagoda is neat, but the aha is slight. → Add one tier whose eave is stamped or cut by a void so the voids differ on each side.
L48 (7/10): The finale intent is only two fills. The statue is almost mirror-symmetric, so a copy-across solve is possible, which is weak for a masterwork. It is 14x14 with big empty corners. → Break the symmetry: make one wing raised and the other folded, and use a void in the plinth so the end test combines void and colour.
L62 (7/10): It repeats L61's 'fill a column and exclude colours' lesson, so a second teach adds little. The cactus is sparse. → Make this the first genuine cross-check: add a column where a colour can only sit in one row. Or merge the two lessons and use the slot for a practise level.
L64 (7/10): Dominated by an 8-wide solid mass of sky colour. The coral mark is tiny, and 71% casual with 35 lines to read is more a chore than an aha. → Make the can body more varied with a stripe, and use the coral rose as the real puzzle.
L71 (7/10): It reads like L63 and L70 with three colours again. Both petal halves are almost the same, which weakens the colour-cross lesson. → Make the two petal halves clearly different, with a missing petal tip and a stamped or void cell, so each side needs its own deduction.
L75 (7/10): Same shape as L73's smudge lesson, with the hidden row solved straight from its crossing columns. The drum is symmetric, so there is little discovery. → Make the hidden row asymmetric (studs at uneven spacing) so every column must be solved separately.
L77 (7/10): A smudged column that is fully solved by one fill row is mechanical. The fireworks are radially symmetric, so a mirror copy is available. → Offset one ray and shorten one spark so the symmetry breaks.
L78 (7/10): At 62% casual, boss difficulty comes mainly from a long smudged column that is solved by overlap on the stripes. It is a bigger step down from L77 than the boss needs. → Add one more anchor row to the stripes so the boss is a challenge but the smudge is still fair, aiming for about 70%.
L79 (7/10): A clean candle, but 89% casual after a boss is only a mild breather, and the sparks are scattered, so the board has empty space. → Tighten the spark halo to a ring of four and enlarge the candle so the board is used better.
L81 (7/10): At 61% casual, the smudged column with scattered fireflies makes this a hard combine with no mid-chapter easing. The 'fireflies at different heights' step needs guesswork-like cross-checking. → Put the fireflies on one clear row pair so the column can be completed in two logical steps. Aim for about 72%.
L83 (7/10): At 59% casual it is harder than the finale after it (55%) only by a small margin, so the sequence has no clear peak. The pinwheel is hard to read with three colours and two folds. → Ease it to about 68% by adding one solid vane edge, and keep L84 as the clear peak.
L86 (7/10): A second teach with the same 'measure between walls' lesson as L85, and the heart mug is hard to read, with 74% casual for a teach. → Give the crack a different job, e.g. a foot that is bounded on one side only by the edge, so the second teach adds something.
L91 (7/10): 90% casual and 97% skilled is an isolated easy spot, and the 'bowl tapering' lesson repeats L87-L88. → Cut to a smaller board or add a stamp; keep it below 90% casual and below 95% skilled.
L92 (7/10): The 'edge columns cap the ?' step is the same as L87. The jar is largely a flat band of cloth, so little is discovered. → Put the crack in the jar body, boxed by lid and label, so the width depends on a colour cross-check.

